  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: Zonal westerly flow prevails across the\nInland Northwest through Friday, maintaining the quiet,\nunimpactful weather pattern. A weak wave embedded in the zonal\nflow will bring passing high clouds regionwide today and low \nchances for showers across the North Cascades. Any showers that \ndevelop over the North Cascades will be very light with chances \nfor measurable precipitation (greater than 0.01 inches) ranging \nfrom 20 to 30 percent. Elsewhere, conditions will remain dry. \nTemperatures today will peak in the mid 60s to low 70s, then \nwill warm by around 5 degrees for Friday, rising into the upper \n60s to upper 70s. While afternoon temperatures will be warm, \nmornings will still feel crisp and fall-like thanks to \nradiational cooling under mostly clear skies and light winds. \nLow temperatures will range from the upper 30s across the \nsheltered northern valleys to the low 50s for the warmest spots \nfurther south including Wenatchee and Lewiston.\n\nSaturday through Monday: An upper level ridge builds over the \nweekend, and as the ridge builds, high temperatures will \ncontinue to climb. Afternoon highs will peak Saturday through \nMonday in the mid 70s to low 80s, 10 to 15 degrees above \nclimatological normals for early October. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Ensembles generally agree on a low\npressure system dropping into the Gulf of Alaska by the middle\nof next week, flattening the upper-level ridge across the Inland\nNorthwest and transitioning the flow pattern back to a zonal\nregime. Increased moisture moving in aloft will bring some \nclouds over the northern mountains, but the forecast remains\ndry. High temperatures will cool slightly, returning to the low\nto upper 70s across the lower elevations. /Fewkes",
